From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: precise frame-cap ack release without the viz busy loop (Windows)

Feature: KccFrameCapPreciseRelease (default off), param spin_us (default
200, clamped to 0..200). Windows only; the flag does not exist on Linux and
macOS and nothing changes there.

What the stock code does
------------------------
The survey expected the CustomFrameCap release timer to fire 0-2 ms late on
Windows because the viz thread waits in whole milliseconds. That is what
would happen if the thread slept, and it does not sleep:

* --disable-frame-rate-limit selects BackToBackBeginFrameSource, driven by
  a DelayBasedTimeSource with a zero interval
  (components/viz/service/frame_sinks/root_compositor_frame_sink_impl.cc,
  RootCompositorFrameSinkImpl::Create).
* DelayBasedTimeSource::OnTimerTick() posts the next tick (an immediate
  task) before it calls the client.
* BackToBackBeginFrameSource::OnTimerTick() starts with
  "if (RequestCallbackOnGpuAvailable()) return;" and so returns before its
  "time_source_->SetActive(false)" whenever the gpu is busy and the one
  BeginFrame allowed after busy has been used.

The frame cap keeps the gpu-busy latch set until it releases the swap ack.
So from the moment the renderer has submitted its frame (which re-arms the
tick through CompositorFrameSinkSupport -> DidFinishFrame) until the ack
release, the VizCompositorThread runs an endless chain of immediate tick
tasks: one core flat out for (cap period - renderer frame time) of every
frame, roughly 75% of a core at a 240 cap and 85% at 144 with a 1 ms frame.
A side effect is that |ack_release_timer_| is serviced within microseconds,
because a thread that never goes idle never reaches the millisecond wait.

When the thread does sleep, the wake-up really is coarse:
MessagePumpDefault::Run() -> WaitableEvent::TimedWaitImpl()
(base/synchronization/waitable_event_win.cc) waits with
"remaining.InMillisecondsRoundedUp()" and expires on the system clock tick,
which ThreadControllerWithMessagePumpImpl::DoIdleWork() raises to 1 ms on
mains and only to 8 ms on battery (base/time/time_win.cc,
kMinTimerIntervalHighResMs / kMinTimerIntervalLowResMs). That is 0-2 ms
late on mains and up to ~8 ms on battery.

What this patch changes
-----------------------
1. components/viz/common/frame_sinks/begin_frame_source.{h,cc}:
   BeginFrameSource::SetParkThrottledBeginFrames(). While set,
   BackToBackBeginFrameSource::OnTimerTick() stops the time source when the
   tick is withheld for gpu-busy, and OnGpuNoLongerBusy() restarts it. The
   restarted tick is a posted task rather than an inline call, because the
   time source drops its tick time when stopped.
2. components/viz/service/display/frame_cap_precise_timer_win.{h,cc} (new):
   a CREATE_WAITABLE_TIMER_HIGH_RESOLUTION waitable timer (Windows 10
   1803+), set with a relative due time and watched with
   base::win::ObjectWatcher::StartWatchingMultipleTimes(), so the viz
   thread is never blocked on it. The kernel expires such a timer on the
   first clock tick at or after the due time, with the clock running at its
   maximum rate (0.5 ms on common hardware), so it is set spin_us before
   the grid time and the viz thread spins for the remainder, never longer
   than spin_us. Notifications are posted from the thread pool and can be
   stale; they are treated as a hint and the clock decides (too early: set
   the timer again; cancelled: ignore).
3. components/viz/service/display/display_scheduler.{h,cc}: the ack release
   goes through StartAckReleaseTimer() / StopAckReleaseTimer() /
   IsAckReleaseTimerRunning(), which use the precise timer when there is
   one and |ack_release_timer_| otherwise or when the OS timer cannot be
   set. Still exactly one armed timer and one
   DidReceiveSwapBuffersAckImpl() per deferred ack;
   FlushPendingSwapAckDelay() and SetFrameCapInterval() cancel whichever is
   armed. Parking is requested only while a cap is in force and the precise
   timer exists.

Net effect with the flag on: under a cap the viz thread sleeps through the
hold and is woken for the release. Expected release error, assuming the
0.5 ms kernel granularity: never early, late by 0 to about 0.35 ms, about
0.12 ms on average with spin_us/200. This is LESS exact than the accidental
spin it replaces (microseconds) and far cheaper; the same on battery as on
mains. With the flag off every call made is the same as before
(|precise_ack_timer_| is null, |tick_parked_| is never set).

Other platforms (not patched)
-----------------------------
The same spin exists on Linux and macOS, so their release is on time too.
Parking needs a wake-up that is better than the pump's:
* Linux/X11: the viz thread runs MessagePumpGlib
  (ui/ozone/platform/x11/ozone_platform_x11.cc sets
  message_pump_type_for_viz_compositor = UI), whose poll timeout is whole
  milliseconds rounded up (GetTimeIntervalMilliseconds), i.e. 0-1 ms late.
  A timerfd is not an option: timerfd_create/settime are in
  SyscallSets::IsAdvancedTimer and crash the sandboxed gpu process with
  SIGSYS (sandbox/linux/seccomp-bpf-helpers/baseline_policy.cc). It would
  need a helper thread; left for a follow-up.
* macOS: NS_RUNLOOP pump, CFRunLoopTimer with zero tolerance. Not
  millisecond-quantised by Chromium; kernel timer coalescing for a
  USER_INTERACTIVE thread could not be established from this tree.

How to A/B
----------
--enable-features=KccFrameCapPreciseRelease (optionally
:spin_us/0 or :spin_us/100) with a cap that the machine can exceed, e.g.
CustomFrameCap:fps/240 or win.setFrameCap(240), against the same run
without it.
* CPU time of VizCompositorThread in the GPU process: should drop from
  most of a core to a few percent.
* Frame pacing: present-to-present interval spread (PresentMon
  msBetweenPresents, or the "DisplayScheduler:pending_swaps" slices in a
  viz trace). Expect a slightly wider spread than without the flag (see
  above) and no change in mean FPS.
* On battery, compare as well: with the flag off the release stays exact
  only because of the spin.

Not verified (nothing was compiled or run)
------------------------------------------
* The 0.5 ms granularity of high-resolution waitable timers is from
  Microsoft's driver documentation ("limited only by the maximum supported
  resolution of the system clock", "never expires early"), not measured.
* Latency of the thread-pool wait -> PostTask -> viz thread hop. It eats
  into spin_us; under full CPU saturation the normal-priority pool thread
  could be delayed, which would delay the release.
* When the cap is not binding (renderer slower than the cap) the viz
  thread now sleeps between swap and swap ack instead of spinning, which
  costs one thread wake-up per frame.

Build and check status (2026-11-21)
  - Compiled without any change on Windows in the Electron 45.4.1 DCHECK
    build (dcheck_always_on).
  - Frame-cap gate with the switch on (test/run-frame-cap-gate.ps1): launch
    caps 60/144/240, setFrameCap at run time, hide/show, resize, a change
    of N, setFrameCap(0) and a second animating view, all within 1 %; a cap
    above reach loses no rate (best of 5 within +0.5..+1.8 % of the switch
    off). test/frame-cap-test.js cpu suite at a 144 cap:
    VizCompositorThread 88 % of a core with the switch off, 16 % with it on
    (2 runs each).
  - Not checked: a GPU swap chain (software compositing here), release
    timing against real vsync hardware.
The "not compiled" / "not verified" notes above were written before the
build; they stay for the caveats they list. Details and raw logs:
patches/electron-45/RESULTS.md and results/.

